-- Sealed bids: commit now, reveal later, and let the record refuse a
-- reveal that differs from what was committed.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain. An auctioneer offers a lot and names the
-- instant bidding closes. Each bidder hands in a sealed envelope before
-- the close: not the bid, but a commitment to it, a digest of the bid
-- with a secret salt only the bidder knows. Nobody, the auctioneer
-- included, can read a bid before the close. After the close each
-- bidder opens their envelope: they reveal the bid and the salt, and
-- anyone can recompute the digest and see that it matches what was
-- handed in. A bidder cannot change their mind after seeing the others,
-- and the auctioneer cannot favour one bidder by leaking the rest.
--
-- The same shape governs a blind review, a forecast that must not be
-- edited after the outcome is known, and any commit-now-reveal-later
-- protocol. What Morpholog adds is that the check is a rule of the
-- record, not a reader's script: a reveal that does not hash to its
-- commitment is not a bid at all, and no act can admit it.
--
-- Two kinds of rule do the work below. An `invariant` is a standing
-- rule about what the record may hold; the runtime refuses any change
-- that would break one. A `require` is a gate on one act, checked at
-- the moment someone acts and never again. "A revealed bid hashes to
-- its commitment" is an invariant: it is true of the record, whoever
-- acts and in whatever order. "You may seal only before the close" is a
-- gate: closing the lot must not reach back and unseat the envelopes
-- that were handed in lawfully while it was open.

program sealed_bids

-- The vocabulary. Every claim is append only: an offered lot, a sealed
-- envelope, a closed lot and a revealed bid are each a thing that
-- happened, and the record never forgets one. A bidder has one envelope
-- and one reveal per lot.
predicate Lot(lot: Subject, auctioneer: Subject, closes_at: Timestamp)
    unique by (lot)
    append only
predicate Sealed(lot: Subject, bidder: Subject, commitment: Subject)
    unique by (lot, bidder)
    append only
predicate Closed(lot: Subject)
    append only
predicate Bid(lot: Subject, bidder: Subject, amount: Decimal, salt: Subject)
    unique by (lot, bidder)
    append only
predicate LeadingBid(lot: Subject, bidder: Subject, amount: Decimal)

-- The commitment. `digest` hashes its arguments in order, each in the
-- encoding the record stores it in, so a bid revealed as `100.0` and a
-- bid sealed as `100` are the same bid. The first argument names the
-- protocol: if a later version of this programme changes what goes
-- into the envelope, it names a new protocol and keeps this rule
-- beside the new one, so an envelope sealed under this one is still
-- opened under this one. The lot and the
-- bidder are in it so an envelope sealed for one lot cannot be opened
-- against another. The salt is in it so a bid cannot be guessed: an
-- amount to the penny under a thousand pounds is only a hundred
-- thousand candidates, and without a salt anyone holding the
-- commitment could try them all before the close. The salt comes from
-- the bidder's own randomness; the record never makes one.
invariant a_revealed_bid_hashes_to_its_commitment:
    Bid(lot, bidder, amount, salt) and Sealed(lot, bidder, commitment)
    implies digest(#sealed_bid_v1, lot, bidder, amount, salt) = commitment

-- A reveal opens an envelope that was handed in, on a lot that has
-- closed. These are rules, not gates: however the claims arrive, the
-- record holds no bid without its envelope and no bid on an open lot.
invariant a_bid_is_of_a_sealed_envelope:
    Bid(lot, bidder, _, _) implies Sealed(lot, bidder, _)

invariant a_bid_stands_on_a_closed_lot:
    Bid(lot, _, _, _) implies Closed(lot)

invariant an_envelope_is_for_an_offered_lot:
    Sealed(lot, _, _) implies Lot(lot, _, _)

-- The highest revealed bid so far. A leader, not a winner: envelopes
-- can still be opened after the close, and the leader can change with
-- each one. An award needs a reveal deadline, which this example does
-- not model.
derived LeadingBid(lot, bidder):
    over (Bid(lot, bidder, bid, _)
          and not (exists other: Bid(lot, other, higher, _) and higher > bid))
    value amount = value Bid(lot: lot, bidder: bidder, amount: _, ..)

-- The auctioneer is whoever offers the lot.
transformation offer(lot, closes_at):
    admit Lot(lot, actor, closes_at)

-- Sealing takes two gates, because a clock is not a seal. `decided_at`
-- is the instant this decision began, and a decision begun before the
-- deadline can commit after it. `Closed` is a claim, and a seal that
-- finds the lot closed is refused whatever its clock said: the record,
-- not the clock, orders every envelope before every reveal. The bidder
-- is the actor, so nobody can hand in an envelope in another's name.
transformation seal(lot, commitment):
    bind offered: Lot(lot, _, closes_at)
    require before_the_deadline: decided_at strictly_before closes_at
    require still_open: not Closed(lot)
    admit Sealed(lot, actor, commitment)

-- Only the auctioneer closes the lot, and only once the deadline has
-- passed.
transformation close(lot):
    bind offered: Lot(lot, actor, closes_at)
    require deadline_passed: closes_at at_or_before decided_at
    require still_open: not Closed(lot)
    admit Closed(lot)

-- Opening the envelope: the bid and the salt, admitted under the
-- bidder's own name. The gate says the lot is closed; the rules above
-- say the rest, and a reveal that does not match its envelope is
-- refused by the first of them.
transformation reveal(lot, amount, salt):
    require closed: Closed(lot)
    admit Bid(lot, actor, amount, salt)
